1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,949
This project treats a machine-learning model like a release.

2
00:00:03,949 --> 00:00:21,584
A fraud detector trained on the public credit-card data set: a Vertex AI pipeline extracts from BigQuery, trains, evaluates, and then an objective gate decides whether the candidate may exist at all; it must clear an absolute floor on A U C P R and it must not regress the current champion.

3
00:00:21,584 --> 00:00:33,320
If it passes, it becomes a version in the Model Registry and goes out as a canary: ten percent of one endpoint's traffic next to the champion, a smoke test through the endpoint, then promotion.

4
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:36,178
After the release the model keeps being judged.

5
00:00:36,178 --> 00:00:50,529
Every prediction request is logged to BigQuery; a scheduled job computes the population stability index of live traffic against the champion's training baseline and rolls traffic back to the champion on its own when a challenger drifts.

6
00:00:50,529 --> 00:00:53,570
Prediction SLOs sit on the endpoint's own metrics.

7
00:00:53,570 --> 00:00:57,948
Everything is Terraform and the pipeline is submitted keylessly from CI.

8
00:00:59,358 --> 00:01:01,339
The pipeline in the Vertex console.

9
00:01:01,339 --> 00:01:08,923
Six components, each a small Python container: extract, train, evaluate, the quality gate, register, and canary deploy with promotion.

10
00:01:08,923 --> 00:01:24,033
This run is a healthy release: the gate compared the candidate against the champion and let it through, the new version was deployed at ten percent beside the old one, smoke-tested through the endpoint, promoted to one hundred percent, and the old version undeployed.

11
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:27,654
Each step's inputs, outputs and metrics are recorded as lineage.

12
00:01:29,772 --> 00:01:38,604
Live predictions through the endpoint: real rows from the public set, including fraud rows, scored by the serving version with the truth label next to each score.

13
00:01:38,604 --> 00:01:47,436
The response names the deployed model and the model version, so every prediction is attributable to a registry entry, and every request lands in the BigQuery log.

14
00:01:49,051 --> 00:01:51,182
The same run from the command line.

15
00:01:51,182 --> 00:01:56,419
The gate's verdict is explicit: the candidate's A U C P R, the champion's, and a pass.

16
00:01:56,419 --> 00:02:06,224
The promote step then writes champion dot json, which the next gate will read, and the baseline of feature distributions that the drift job will compare against.

17
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:11,035
Nothing in this path is a human clicking approve; the numbers are the approval.

18
00:02:12,639 --> 00:02:21,205
The Model Registry holds every version under one parent model, with the evaluation metrics as labels and the aliases champion and challenger.

19
00:02:21,205 --> 00:02:28,556
The endpoint page shows what is actually serving: one endpoint, the deployed version, its traffic share and its replicas.

20
00:02:28,556 --> 00:02:32,930
A version in the registry is an artifact; a deployed model is a release.

21
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:34,935
Keeping those apart is the point.

22
00:02:37,043 --> 00:02:38,364
The bad-model drill.

23
00:02:38,364 --> 00:02:46,618
I submit the same pipeline with sabotage switched on: a third of the labels shuffled and the three strongest features zeroed.

24
00:02:46,618 --> 00:02:58,505
Training still succeeds, evaluation still produces numbers, and the gate refuses it with the reason spelled out: the A U C P R collapsed below the floor and far below the champion.

25
00:02:58,505 --> 00:03:03,458
The run is red, the registry has no new version, the endpoint is untouched.

26
00:03:03,458 --> 00:03:08,939
That is a worse model that never reached production, caught by a rule, not by luck.

27
00:03:10,542 --> 00:03:11,411
The drift drill.

28
00:03:11,411 --> 00:03:13,963
A challenger is serving ten percent of traffic.

29
00:03:13,963 --> 00:03:23,849
I send three hundred requests whose feature distribution is nothing like the training data, amounts forty times higher, the first four components shifted, and then run the drift job.

30
00:03:23,849 --> 00:03:33,300
It reads the last hour of logged requests, bins them with the champion's baseline, and the population stability index jumps far above the threshold on exactly those features.

31
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:40,470
Because a challenger was serving, the job moves one hundred percent of traffic back to the champion and the alert fires for a human.

32
00:03:42,574 --> 00:03:44,950
Observability for a model, the SRE way.

33
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:53,601
The dashboard shows predictions and errors per deployed model, p ninety-five model latency, replicas, and the PSI series the drift job writes.

34
00:03:53,601 --> 00:04:04,629
Two SLOs on the endpoint's own metrics: ninety-nine point five percent of predictions succeed and ninety-five percent finish under three hundred milliseconds, with burn-rate alerts.

35
00:04:04,629 --> 00:04:08,710
A model that is accurate but slow or erroring is still an incident.

36
00:04:10,597 --> 00:04:19,631
Delivery is keyless: a push to the pipeline folder compiles the definition and submits a release run as the pipeline service account through Workload Identity Federation.

37
00:04:19,631 --> 00:04:26,965
Run workflow with sabotage set to true ships the bad model on purpose, so the gate can be exercised from the same place as a real release.

38
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:44,715
Everything shown is in the repository: the pipeline with its six components and the gate, the drift job with PSI and the rollback, Terraform for the endpoint, logging, SLOs, alerts, dashboard, identities and federation, the drills and the keyless workflow.

39
00:04:44,715 --> 00:04:50,036
The idle cost is the endpoint's replicas, and the teardown script removes it all.

40
00:04:50,036 --> 00:04:51,349
Thanks for watching.
